          14
                                 CHAPTER 3

      THE WISDOM OF PRODUCTIVITY

The Law of Value

A person is esteemed valuable when they have something of value they
add to others. Your Value describes everything you have, you are, or
you do that solves problems for others, puts you in useful demand and
attracts a reward towards you. It is any trait, skill, ability, personality,
qualification or grace that you possess that is beneficial to others and
can meet their felt needs. Value can be spiritual, physical, emotional,
intellectual, academic, or financial. It includes character, material
possessions, gifts, skills, anointing, grace, certifications, expertise and
strength of character. Simply put, value describes what you have that
you can exchange for profit.

In the marketplace of life, your value creates your reward. Whenever
life wants to reward with success, a need is created that one is equipped
to meet. Sustainable success comes when one responds profitably to
the needs which one is equipped to meet. The first question in the
quest for success is, ‘what value do I have to offer people’?

In 2 Kings 4, a widow met Prophet Elisha because she was in a dire

                                                              15
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

situation. Her deceased husband left her with a huge debt and two
children to fend for. The creditors came and were about to seize the
children as collateral for the loan. In providing a solution to this
pathetic case, Elisha directed her to the wisdom of identifying and
deploying value. He asked her, ‘‘What can I do to help you?" Elisha
asked. "Tell me, what do you have in the house?" 2 Kings 4:2. God’s
help to you will ultimately place a demand on what you have to offer.
Real poverty is not having no material possession; it is having nothing
to offer. People who have nothing in their hands but have something to
offer will eventually have something in their hands. One will not remain
poor forever who has mastered the art of offering value in a profitable
way.
The woman in the story above made a mistake most poor folks make,
she replied to the Prophet, ‘I have nothing at all . . .’ However, she later
realised her error and promptly corrected it by adding, ‘Except a flask
of olive oil.’ The really poor person is one who thinks and believes
he/she have nothing to offer.

Sell Your Value

The widow in the scripture used the oil she had and it supernaturally

multiplied. Value multiplies when you deploy it. She exited failure into

success, when the man of God instructed her to go ahead and sell the

multiplied oil. According to 2 Kings 4:7, Elisha said, "Now sell the olive

oil and pay your debts, and you and your sons can live on what is left

over." The prophet instructed her to sell the oil, meaning God is not

against productivity, enterprise and profitability. Why did not God, in

all his benevolence, instruct her to give the oil out for free to her

hungry neighbours?  16
                                      THE WISDOM OF PRODUCTIVITY

 It is because God loves and blesses enterprise and profitability. God
does not have anything against you monetising your value and making
financial profit out of it. God’s only aversion to enterprise is when
merchandise was made of spiritual things in the House of God to make
money off people’s sacrifices and service to God (John 2: 13 - 17).

What you Have is the Seed for What you Want

The Biblical account of the call and commissioning of Moses also
shows that God uses what you have as the seed for what you want.
Your value is the seed for your success. God had appeared to Moses in
the burning bush and sent him to liberate the nation of Israel in Egypt.
Moses felt inadequate and wanted to know how he would succeed in
such a great task despite his many deficiencies. In Exo. 4: 2, The Lord
asked him, What is that in thine hand? And he said, A rod. It was the rod
that God used to do miracles, signs and wonders throughout the
lifetime of Moses. In our contemporary context, you may not be
holding a literal shepherd’s rod like Moses did but the point still
remains that God will place a demand on something He has already
placed within you for your success. Therefore, your personal success is
in what you have, not what another person has. This is the reason,
living a life of copying what others are doing is not wise. People that try
to success by entirely copying others end up as a duplicate instead of
an original. We succeed in life by learning from others not by copying
them. Stay with God to know our individual calling and assignment.
Discover and maximise your uniqueness. Focus and use what God has
given you and do not be consumed about what He has given someone
else. The seed of your success and greatness is inside you.

                                                              17
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

Additionally, in Matt 14: 14 - 21, when Jesus Christ wanted to feed five
