                                                              41
                                 CHAPTER 5

            THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS
                          AND NETWORKS

This WISDOM FOR LIVING Book will end by examining another
important factor in attaining sustainable success. Which is the wisdom
of building advantageous relationships and networks.

The Power of Relationships

One most singular human factor in the pursuit of success and
fulfillment are the relationships in one’s life. The people around you in
a large way determines how far you will go in life. To know where you
are headed, look at where the people closest to you seems to be
headed. If you need to change direction and results in life, the first step
would be to change the relationships in your life.
In attaining success, a major area of investment is into advantageous
relationships. The skills of managing relationships and creating
advantageous affiliations and networks are great success asset.

Types of Relationships

There are different types of relationships. All people are not the same
to you and you must never relate to them as such. You must learn to
define and demarcate different relationships for different areas of your
life.

                                                              42
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

Different types of relationship include:

     Family Relationships
     Friendship Relationships
     Kingdom/Ministry Relationships
     Business and Financial Relationships
     Professional Relationships
     Romantic Relationships
     Marital Relationship
     Enmity relationships.
. . . Note that this is not an exclusive list.

It is important to limit one’s interaction with people to the basis of the
relationship that exist with them. If one is ever going to allow some
people at a relationship type with one to cross to another type, it must
be solely on the basis that they have passed the test for suitability for
the new type of relationship. For example, a work colleague has a
professional relationship with you and need to pass certain vital tests
before you discuss your family issues with them. Otherwise, limit your
relationships with them to professional issues.

A common relationship mistake people make is not to understand,
define and limit interaction with people to the type of relationship that
exists between them. This is the reason bosses sleep with their
secretaries, work colleagues get into romantic relationships with each
other, business partners share personal secrets which they eventually
trade as weapons against each other when things go sour. It is amazing
that some people are unaware of the the fact that because somebody
has a common business interest with them does not mean the person
deserves to be their confidant on personal matters. Lack of
understanding of the limits and extents of the different types of
relationship is the reason ‘friends’ defraud of money. Wisdom makes
one understand that a friendship is 4d3ifferent from a business
                        THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS AND NETWORKS

relationship. The fact that you are friends with someone does not mean
they are suited to do business together with you. Family relationship is
also different from work or business relationship. So, when you employ
people solely because they are your cousins or siblings that need a job,
you might be making a great mistake. The truth is that both you and
the family member might be better off if you employ someone else and
refer him/her to another employer. Also, friendship is different from a
romantic and/or marital relationship. Therefore, because you have a
friend of the opposite gender that you get along well with does not
mean you should feel ‘led’ to marry them. There are many confusions
and problems people get into because they do not understand how to
correctly place and strictly manage people based on the types of
relationships they belong.

Of course, it should be noted that a person can cross from a type of
relationship to another. In fact, someone can occupy more than one
type of relationship with you. However, this must be deliberately
evaluated, assessed and consciously entered. You should allow people
to cross from one type of relationship to another if you have discovered
them suitable and consciously decided that they enter to that additional
type of relationship. Therefore, for example, it is not impossible that
work colleagues may eventually marry each other. However, this
should be because they mutually discovered the desired quality of a
suitable spouse in each other during the course of their work together,
and not merely because the proximity of work influenced an emotional
affiliation that they mistook that for a marital compatibility. Generally,
roles, demands and personal attributes that make people effective as
work partners are not necessarily be the ones that will be needed to
complement each other as marriage partners.

Three Directions of Relationships

Generally, there are three directions towards which we relate with

people.  44
                        THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS AND NETWORKS

We are either relating with people above us, people at our level or
people below us. It is humility to know that someone is above us in
certain areas of life. It is equally not pride to know that someone is
below us. However, whether someone is above, below or at the same
level with us in life, we must relate with them in humility and accord
them honour. According to 1 Peter 2:17, honor all people. love the
brotherhood (the Christian family of believers). Fear God. Honor the
king. We must treat all people with honour and respect whether they
are above, below or at our level.

The three directions of relationships are:

Up-Line/Vertical Relationship: There are people who have been placed
above us in the hierarchical structure of life. Examples are parents,
bosses at work, mentors, teachers, pastors, superiors or seniors in a
system. We are to accord honour to all people in our relations with
them. However, when someone is above us, we owe them particular
honour in deference to them. According to Rom. 1: 7, Render therefore
to all their dues: tribute to whom tribute is due; custom to whom custom;
fear to whom fear; honour to whom honour. There is the generic honour
code we must apply in relating with all people. But, there are some
specific others that when we honour them, we are giving them their
due. 1 Tim 5:17 says, Let the elders that rule well be counted worthy of
double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine. This
shows that elders (spiritual leaders and elderly people by age) are
deserving of honour. This scripture extends to mean that anyone that is
teaching, raising and building you in any regards, (academic,
professionally and most especially in the Word of God) is deserving of
a higher level of honour than you accord people generically. For this
reason, teachers and lecturers are deserving of honour from their
students and pastors, more especially, are worthy of honour from the
people they lead.

Horizontal/Side-Ways Relationship: There are people at the same level
with you in life. This includes friend45s, colleagues and partners.
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

The foundation principle for all relationships is honour. Therefore, we
must honour people at the same level with us. When we have people at
the same level, we should be committed to their progress and support
them. Let it be evident to your friends, partners and colleagues that you
are not in competition with them but are committed to their success
and you expect them to be committed to yours. As much as lies in your
power, make them know they are your team-mates and not opponents.

Down-line/Vertical Relationship: Everyone living a worthwhile life must
have someone that they are raising. Down-lines include proteges,
younger siblings, subordinates, juniors, children, employees etc.
Remember, the foundational code for all relationships is honour.
Therefore, the people under you equally deserve your honour. As a
superior, the only authority you actually have over your down-lines is
the honour they have for you. In other words, as a leader, the only right
you have over people is their honour of you. If you lose that honour,
you will see them walk away from you, then you will realise more than
ever that their being under you was because of submission and honour
out of volition.

Honour is actually reciprocal. The only way to get honour is to give it.
Therefore, one must be most careful not to dishonour someone who
honours one. A job employment relationship between a boss and an
employee is usually the most non-volition of relationships. However,
even at that, we have seen people walk away from a job because they
felt dishonoured.

Another key to relating with subordinates is to show commitment to
their progress. People below in relationship should have a value or
service they are rendering to the superior or else, they are unnecessary
in his/her life. Down-lines often miss the fact that their only access to a
higher person is the service or value they are giving to him. A great
person has so many people seeking4h6 is attention that he/she may have
                        THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS AND NETWORKS

no time for you except their is a role or value you are adding to
him/her. The flip side is that the superior must be verbally and
evidently interested and committed to their life progress of the
subordinates as well. If downlines feel you are merely using them to
achieve your end without consideration for their life, personal progress
or life vision. They will only be with you as long as it is a means of
survival for them and at that they can’t wait to get out without looking
back.

The Honour Code

Honour involves recognising, appreciating and rewarding a person’s
worth and uniqueness. Honour should be according to all people
irrespective of the direction of their relationship with you. This means
whether superior, colleague or subordinate, the value of people must be
discerned and appreciated. Dishonouring is a sin - When someone is
playing a role or contributing a value and you think what they are
doing is not really important. The driver is important, he is driving. It
is not everyone that can drive safely. The gate-man is important, he is
probably doing something you don’t want to do. Your cleaner is
important, if he is not there and you have to do the cleaner yourself, it
will take time and effort from you. It is a heart of dishonour that makes
one believe that he can dispose of anyone and replace them without
any wink. Truly, there is no one that is indispensable or irreplaceable.
However, it is not easy to find good people so honour every good
person around you no matter their status or level.

It is important to note the fact that you are paying someone to do
something does not mean you should not accord them honour for what
they do. Honour involves showing people respect foremost because the
are human beings like you, then secondly for the value they add.
Honour has a tone, a demeanor, a countenance and attitude. It is
important to speak respectfully to people. If you live in a culture where
they are appellations and gestures f4o7r showing respect, use them for
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

people irrespective of their social status.
Other important principles of honour include the law of appreciation
and reward. Appreciation and reward should be done verbally, in
actions and in giving. When dealing with superiors, honour involves
going the extra mile and making sacrifices in service. You can claim to
honour someone above you and serve them at convenience. Honour
involves giving substance to the person you want to show honour to.

Seven Levels of General Relationships

There are different type of relationships and there are different levels of
relationships. Another relationship mistake akin to relationship to
with people on one type based on another type is relating with people
on one level of relationship based on another level.
The following are levels of relationships:

Stranger - This is someone you do not really know, have not met before
or do not have a relationship with. When you meet a stranger, you do
not owe them access to your person or information. What you owe
strangers is to show them God’s love, be kind and give help to them. It
is absolute foolishness to meet someone in a vehicle for the first time
and you go off with information about your self, family, vision, work
etc. People have met people in vehicles and because there a seeming
sweet flow of conversation, they shared vital personal information. In
fact, some there and then proposed marriage and believed they fell in
love at first sight. Life information is not for a stranger level of
relationship.

Acquaintance - This is someone you know but do not have a vital
relationship with in a real sense. Neighbours, church members, school
mates could be in this level. It is unwise to relate with acquaintances as
if they are strangers. In addition to God’s love that you owe all men,
you must show courtesy, polite and kindness to acquaintances and give

                                                              48
                        THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS AND NETWORKS

them the honour of recognition. While it may be okay not to give
strangers a ride in some parts of the world due to security reason, it is
not right to see a neighbour, church member or an acquaintance who is
stuck and drive pass as if you do not know them. However, an
acquaintance does not deserve information about your person or
secrets. The only difference between an acquaintance and a stranger is
recognition. You really do not know them, you only recognise them, so
be cautious.

Fellow: Fellows are people who are for what you are also for. They are
with you because of a common course that join you both together.
Fellows are vital parts of your success and are great assets to you so
you must value and give your best to them. However, fellows are not
loyal to you, they are loyal to the mutual course you all pursue.
Therefore, you cannot commit your life to Fellows. Fellows are not to
be your life confidants and personal secrets are not for them. There are
not interested in you, they are interested in what you are interested in.
When dealing with Fellows, you need to wisely guard your personal
interest so that you will not eventually be at the losing end of life.

Comrade: These are people who are against what you are against. An
old adage says, ‘the enemy of my enemy is my friend.’ This is quite far
from the truth, the enemy of your enemy is actually a comrade.A
comrade is also with you because of a common goal however, they are
not loyal to you. If you join the other side of the common course, you
become an enemy immediately. In fact, if the common enemy - which
can be a system, an ideology, the government, a person or an evil, is
defeated, you may be their next target. You do not owe comrades
anything but inputs into the common course. You may need comrades
to fight evil but be very careful of comrades; never put yourself into the
hand of a comrade.

Close Relationship/Friendship: A frie49nd is interested in you as a person
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

and give relational preference or deference to you above others.
Anyone that relates with you the way he/she relates with every other
person is not a friend. He could be a acquaintance, comrade or
constituent. Friendship demands support and commitment to the
person beyond the course, from both party. Many of the people people
call friends are not friends, they fall into one or other of the categories
above. When it comes to true friendships, every person has the
emotional and heart bandwidth to accommodate just a few.

Inner Circle: They are people who you trust enough to let them know
what is going on in your personal space and life. This level of
relationship is earned through trust and loyalty. The higher the level of
relationship, the greater the danger of having an unfit person in it.
Among your friends and family, only few should be admitted into your
life’s inner circle based on proven love, loyalty and dependability over
time. Your inner circle are your life’s line of defense and are also the
ones in the greatest position of advantage to destroy you if they decide
to. Having a wrong person in your inner circle is not just a mistake, it is
a disaster. A spouse is automatically in your inner circle, this is the
reason choosing a wrong marital relationship is dangerous.

Confidant: This is the voice that you have trusted that you can open
everything about yourself to and know that he/she will not take
advantage of you, look down or you, but rather will cover, build and
support you. Any level of relationship from friendship upward must be
earned. A confidant level of relationship must be earned through
utmost trust, loyalty and sacrifice. Your confidant should be mature
(spiritually, emotional and mentally), must be someone who is sincerely
interested in your well-being and progress and has proven it through
sacrifices for you, a person you have proven love for you. This level of
relationship is earned after you have tested and proven the person’s
maturity in handling crucial information, unfeigned love and security
with your progress, genuine concer5n0 and sympathy in your
                        THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS AND NETWORKS

pains, and willingly to cover your faults and wrongs. There is no
relationship type that automatically bestows this level of relationship
on any one from you. Making someone your confidant should be your
personal and willful choice. No one has the right to demand or think
they have positional right (spiritual or secular) to be your confidant,
this place in your life must be earned. If you married right, your spouse
should be a confidant. However, not everyone has this right foundation
in place.

Jesus on the Levels of Relationship

Having established the principles guiding the different types and levels
of relationships, it is important to give a scriptural background for
them. So them these timeless truths would not be seen as fabrication
opinions or the author’s sentiments.
Jesus had people at all levels of relationships with him and He dealt
different with them. There were always the crowd following Jesus, He
loved them, healed them and ministered to them but never committed
himself to them. They were mostly strangers and acquaintances.
John 2:23-25 says, now when He was in Jerusalem at the Passover,
during the feast, many believed in His name when they saw the signs which
He did. But Jesus did not commit Himself to them, because He knew all
men, and had no need that anyone should testify of man, for He knew
what was in man.

Jesus also had people who were fellows and comrades. In Matt 2: 23 -
34, the Sadducee Council attacked Jesus with a tricky question on
resurrection of the death. After Jesus silenced them with a profound
answer, the Pharisees who had always been vocally opposed to Jesus,
were happy and commended Him. This was because they were also on
opposite grounds with the Sadducees on resurrection and Jesus’
answer seems to favour their stand. However, they were not friends and
Jesus never leaned towards them. They were comrades on this

                                                              51
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

particular occasion. A comrade is not for you, don’t commit yourself to
him/her.

Jesus referred to His disciples as his friends. They were always with
Him and believed in Him. In John 15:14-15, He said, Ye are my friends,
if ye do whatsoever I command you. Henceforth I call you not servants;
for the servant knoweth not what his lord doeth: but I have called you
friends; for all things that I have heard of my Father I have made known
unto you. Friends are the ones that deserve access to your person, ways
and information. However, even this is to different degrees.

On several occasions, Jesus would want to do or say something and
would excuse all the other friends and allow only Peter, James and
John access to this information (Matt 17:1, Mk 5: 37, Mk 14: 3) . This
would look like absolutely partially unless one understand that there
are information about you that is for the inner circle and not for every
other friend. Peter, James and John were Jesus’ inner circle when He
walked the earth. Among all Jesus’ friends, John believed he had a
special place and called himself the Beloved and would ask for access
to information that others were not privy to. If Jesus did not give
everyone equal access to His person, you would not be wise to.

General Relationship Tips:

     You are to love everyone but you are not to trust everyone at every
     level of relationship. In relationship love is unconditional but trust
     is conditional. Agape love is to be given freely but trust is to be
     earned.
     Never give all people equal access to your person. Relate with
     people based on the level of relationship they are in with you. This
     is the wisdom for avoiding betrayals, hurts and pains in relating
     with people.
     The level of relationship you ar5e2with a person should be
                        THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS AND NETWORKS

determined by the perceived level of the person to the relationship with
you and not your perceived level of relationship with the person.
Therefore, be a master at letting people dictate where they stand with
you.

     Relationships are investments. The level of investiture of yourself
     into a relationship is what determines what you get out of it. Be
     careful not to waste your investments. Every beneficial relationship
     will make demands on you. There is no point seeking a
     relationship, you are not willing to give the demands it takes. So,
     do not seek or accept a level of relationship that you are not yet
     ready to meet up with the demands or commitments of that level.
     Having a level of relationship with a person is not compulsory.

     Beneficial relationships must be intentionally pursued. There are
     people that are worth having in your life and will bring an
     advantage into your life. It is your responsibility to learn the laws
     that bring beneficial relationships to you. An example of such a law
     is shown in Prov. 18: 24; A man that hath friends must shew
     himself friendly: and there is a friend that sticketh closer than a
     brother .

     Never forget that the average cross-section/survey your close and
     frequent relationships gives a good representation of who you are.
     The overview of the people around you is also a good prediction of
     your future. You can change your future by changing your
     associations/relationships.

     Be henceforth be intentional in selecting and determining the
     relationships in and around your life.

     A key foundation in relationship is that you must maintain your
     self-esteem and self-worth in all relationships. Don’t be need driven
     in any relationship irrespective5o3f the direction. Rather, be value
                                               WISDOM FOR LIVING

addition driven.
     Don’t Hang on to a relationship that is hurting, hurtful and non-
     beneficial. Life is too short for you to expend it on the wrong
     people. If a relationship is damaging and disadvantageous, it is not
     worth you life and time. In every person with a healthy sense of
     self-esteem in a relationship, there is the mind that is willing to
     release and let go if need be. Matt 10:39 says “If you cling to your
     life, you will lose it; but if you give it up (for me), you will save it.
     This applied in relationships as well. Sometimes, the ability to let
     go freely is a sign of self-security nd self-esteem.
     There are no perfect people in life. In all relationships, you will
     need to accommodate people’s inadequacies. While it is important
     to identify the weaknesses and strengths of people you are in
     relationship with, you should not major on their weaknesses.
     Support people to grow in their areas of weakness, remember you
     also have some weakness as well. Commend people’s strength and
     encourage them. No one likes it when you always mention and
     dwell on their area of inadequacy.

                                                                54
                        THE WISDOM OF RELATIONSHIPS AND NETWORKS

